      <data key="d3">The planned annual premium of $12,000 contributes to building the account value over time, which directly affects the total payout under Option II by increasing the accumulation component of the death benefit.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ebbf5cd2-4a0e-4c96-a21c-71b0ce22c1d2" target="3ee62998-6a05-40f4-921c-b0c4be892b48">
      <data key="d3">The planned annual premium of $12,000 aligns with the terms set by selecting Death Benefit Option II, which defines how benefits are paid out at death. This suggests that the premium level supports the coverage type chosen.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ebbf5cd2-4a0e-4c96-a21c-71b0ce22c1d2" target="f655db57-9079-41c0-80b8-f555479b92bd">
      <data key="d3">The planned annual premium of $12,000 contributes to funding the policy's investment component, including allocations into sub-accounts designed to hold mutual fund shares.</data>
      <data key="d4">funding source</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ebbf5cd2-4a0e-4c96-a21c-71b0ce22c1d2" target="d75d6f5b-d1c8-41ad-b706-f720effdd433">
      <data key="d3">The planned annual premium of $12,000 contributes to the investment accounts where Accumulation Units are held before distribution. These units represent the portion of premiums invested in sub-accounts.</data>
      <data key="d4">funding source</data>
