      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">sub account</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="e625d289-2d3f-4555-8f77-cca405189a0b">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">amount</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="c96d9ac8-0ae6-48e3-8b89-2af5261d516c">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">rate</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="105973f8-8ff8-4330-991e-b017e2b3eaaf">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">amount</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="3b6760c8-9a79-47fb-8e41-424cb0985512">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">calculation</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="5f65440f-af02-4252-9aa7-a74e66e1b142">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">percentage range</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="33efe2e6-ba75-467f-8cc4-89425c4f277e">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">charge schedule</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="f32fd9d4-462b-417e-9c50-f00a3ce78b9b">
      <data key="d3">The Face Amount at Risk is used to determine the variable portion of the Mortality &amp; Expense Risk Charge, which is deducted daily from the policyholder’s account.</data>
      <data key="d4">used in calculation</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="af4449ec-8861-4771-a54e-e9486c4a72d4">
      <data key="d3">To compute the Cost of Insurance, one must first determine the Face Amount at Risk, defined as the difference between the Death Benefit and the Account Value.</data>
      <data key="d4">used in</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="43e402cf-d77e-4d32-a1ae-d4caa820bbf9">
      <data key="d3">The definition of Face Amount at Risk relies on the Death Benefit, which under Option I remains constant. Thus, the stability of the death benefit in Option I influences how Face Amount at Risk behaves over time.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="63433ceb-81a0-44ea-95a1-c6bc9c2ae0de">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Face Amount at Risk, defined as Death Benefit minus Account Value, determines the exposure used to calculate variable insurance costs and influences the structure of Option II’s variable death benefit, where higher account values increase potential payouts.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="fc50e6da-a35a-4b0b-b4b6-a838415c3d5c">
      <data key="d3">The account value's fluctuation based on investment performance impacts the face amount at risk, since it is subtracted from the death benefit to determine coverage exposure.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="fa381acf-443b-4930-8921-e02cc04b1150">
      <data key="d3">The face amount at risk decreases when the account value rises under Option II, since it's calculated as death benefit minus account value. Thus, growing account value reduces exposure.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="67ae83fb-0cf5-44ec-b533-6271ab72c41e">
      <data key="d3">The face amount at risk depends on the death benefit, which decreases when partial withdrawals occur. Thus, withdrawals impact the metric defined in fact_sentence_1 by reducing both the death benefit and the calculated risk exposure.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="dc514d96-9b2a-40a5-941c-7335baaddd7f">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Face Amount at Risk underpins the proportional reduction in coverage during partial withdrawals, making it essential for determining how fees like the $50 processing charge affect policy value and future liabilities.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="d7bbd6d3-1f46-4341-bd8b-0214db62b50d">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Face Amount at Risk, calculated as Death Benefit minus Account Value, explains how the variable nature of the death benefit under Option II leads to non-guaranteed outcomes tied to investment performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="d4b0a171-5ef3-4669-b3a4-2f4f8afafc3d">
      <data key="d3">Partial withdrawals decrease the face amount, thereby altering the Death Benefit portion of the formula in fact_sentence_1, leading to a reduced Face Amount at Risk.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="0f79fac3-4cc7-4db8-9988-95a22ef09fff">
      <data key="d3">The calculation of Face Amount at Risk impacts the cost of insurance and indirectly informs the rationale behind surrender charges on partial withdrawals, especially when such actions alter the policy’s risk profile and account balance.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="b84d9a61-e856-449c-a1b7-ffd3b834c5a7">
      <data key="d3">The face amount at risk, defined by subtracting the account value from the death benefit, directly impacts the cost-of-insurance charges and reflects the net exposure covered by the policy. As the death benefit under Option II fluctuates with sub-account performance, so too does the face amount at risk, creating dynamic financial implications tied to investment results.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="1d7528cf-578b-4c9e-805a-b6d7077a310f">
      <data key="d3">A decline in account value increases the face amount at risk, raising insurance costs and reducing cash value reserves, which heightens the likelihood of policy lapse during periods of poor investment performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="522644d7-e290-4908-b13c-663896d15b1c" target="82754332-e72e-42c2-8178-8d00ac6d8b55">
      <data key="d3">The concept of Face Amount at Risk, derived from the Account Value, directly affects the cost-of-insurance burden. If the Account Value declines significantly, it increases the Face Amount at Risk relative to the Death Benefit, raising insurance costs and increasing the chance that the account cannot sustain monthly deductions, leading to potential policy lapse.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
