    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="d718d2dd-4dc4-4503-a75d-71ee2866b3bf">
      <data key="d3">Option I offers a stable death benefit equal to the face amount, while also featuring reduced fees relative to Option II, making it potentially more cost-effective.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="ff1a79b3-08d5-49d1-a8e3-3b8a90ed15c2">
      <data key="d3">The level death benefit under Option I remains constant because it does not include any mechanism for growth through investments.</data>
      <data key="d4">no growth</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="63433ceb-81a0-44ea-95a1-c6bc9c2ae0de">
      <data key="d3">Option I offers a fixed death benefit based only on the face amount, while Option II enhances that benefit by adding the current account value, allowing it to grow with investments.</data>
      <data key="d4">contrasts with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="0a3e0c50-8fc9-4679-94bb-856bff774ce7">
      <data key="d3">Sentence 1 outlines the characteristics of Option I (level death benefit), while Sentence 2 indicates that Option II is actively chosen, implying that the conditions of Option I do not apply under current circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d4">contrasts with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="aa2bafea-758d-4319-863f-08d9cd460b15">
      <data key="d3">Option I offers a guaranteed constant death benefit, whereas Option II involves higher fees, reflecting its more complex structure and potential for variable returns.</data>
      <data key="d4">comparative</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="6e4a5644-f48d-427f-ba8d-2997378c8b42">
      <data key="d3">The presence of Option I, which offers a guaranteed level death benefit, allows the policyholder to exercise flexibility by choosing to switch to Option II under different conditions or preferences.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="fa381acf-443b-4930-8921-e02cc04b1150">
      <data key="d3">Option I offers a stable death benefit unchanged over time, while Option II allows the benefit to increase with account performance, illustrating how each choice differs in long-term payout behavior.</data>
      <data key="d4">contrasts with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="2248cf41-5aff-417f-8516-4b5df05a6c56">
      <data key="d3">The existence of a level death benefit under Option I sets the baseline for the policy’s guaranteed payout, which becomes relevant when considering changes to coverage type, particularly since switching options requires meeting minimum face amount thresholds.</data>
      <data key="d4">conditions</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="d7bbd6d3-1f46-4341-bd8b-0214db62b50d">
      <data key="d3">Option I ensures a stable, guaranteed death benefit, whereas Option II’s benefit varies based on investment performance and lacks guarantees, illustrating how one choice offers certainty while the other introduces uncertainty.</data>
      <data key="d4">contrasts with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="b84d9a61-e856-449c-a1b7-ffd3b834c5a7">
      <data key="d3">Option I ensures a constant death benefit regardless of financial market conditions, contrasting with Option II’s dynamic benefit linked to sub-account returns.</data>
      <data key="d4">contrasts with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="c77d0c8c-e192-4a05-988c-04e682d69d67">
      <data key="d3">The stable death benefit in Option I reflects a choice where guarantees are maintained despite underlying investment risk being transferred to the policyowner.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6">
      <data key="d3">The Level Death Benefit under Option I is reduced dollar-for-dollar by any policy loans taken, meaning each loan directly diminishes the guaranteed payout.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f" target="d4b0a171-5ef3-4669-b3a4-2f4f8afafc3d">
      <data key="d3">The Level Death Benefit in Option I is directly affected by partial withdrawals, which reduce the face amount proportionally, thereby lowering the guaranteed payout upon death.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
