      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">charge</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="9eafaab8-8346-4d69-985f-8687f794e481">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">reduction</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="decd6c86-36db-45ea-88cb-14b69bb2fa9f">
      <data key="d3">Any reduction in the death benefit due to loans applies equally to the Level Death Benefit, ensuring the payout reflects outstanding debt against the policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">applies to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="0a3e0c50-8fc9-4679-94bb-856bff774ce7">
      <data key="d3">The rule that each loan reduces the death benefit dollar-for-dollar applies precisely because the policyholder has opted into Option II, which governs how financial adjustments impact the death benefit.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="63433ceb-81a0-44ea-95a1-c6bc9c2ae0de">
      <data key="d3">Since each loan reduces the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, this directly affects the computation of the variable death benefit outlined in Option II, which ties the benefit to the account value plus the face amount.</data>
      <data key="d4">causes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="fa381acf-443b-4930-8921-e02cc04b1150">
      <data key="d3">Since loans reduce the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, they counteract the potential increase in the death benefit driven by favorable account performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="74e421cc-1b7d-4755-b72f-7b9f5f6bbfe9">
      <data key="d3">Every loan taken from the policy subtracts equally from the death benefit. Because the account value under the Variable option is not guaranteed, such reductions could further jeopardize the policy if combined with poor investment returns.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="d7bbd6d3-1f46-4341-bd8b-0214db62b50d">
      <data key="d3">Since the death benefit under Option II is not guaranteed, any reduction caused by policy loans—such as direct dollar-for-dollar decreases—is consistent with the inherent volatility and flexibility of the variable component.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="fc50e6da-a35a-4b0b-b4b6-a838415c3d5c">
      <data key="d3">Since each loan reduces the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, the extent of such reductions depends on the current account value, which itself fluctuates with sub-account performance.</data>
      <data key="d4">modified by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="b84d9a61-e856-449c-a1b7-ffd3b834c5a7">
      <data key="d3">Policy loans reduce the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, which affects the very variable component tied to sub-account performance mentioned earlier.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="c77d0c8c-e192-4a05-988c-04e682d69d67">
      <data key="d3">The direct reduction of the death benefit by loans reflects the policyowner’s responsibility for managing risks associated with leveraging policy value, reinforcing that investment outcomes rest on personal decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d4">result of</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="1d7528cf-578b-4c9e-805a-b6d7077a310f">
      <data key="d3">Taking a loan reduces the death benefit immediately and permanently. This reduction lowers the policy's overall value, making it more vulnerable to lapse if investment performance remains poor and further depletion occurs.</data>
      <data key="d4">contributes to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="82754332-e72e-42c2-8178-8d00ac6d8b55">
      <data key="d3">Loans directly decrease the death benefit, thereby lowering the overall account value. Since a low account balance raises the risk of not covering monthly fees, each loan heightens the possibility of policy lapse.</data>
      <data key="d4">increases risk of</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="6609aab0-8bef-493f-a6c7-30042cfc3b43">
      <data key="d3">Since every loan reduces the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, the total possible reduction depends on how large the loan is relative to the surrender value. Therefore, capping loans at 90% of surrender value limits the maximum potential decrease in the death benefit.</data>
      <data key="d4">limited by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="f1fab3de-ee39-499b-b5bb-1d9134b0517d">
      <data key="d3">Each policy loan reduces the death benefit by an equal dollar amount, irrespective of the 5.5% interest charged on the loan balance. This ensures the insurer’s liability diminishes linearly with borrowed amounts.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="36ea11ef-2773-4656-b8f6-b515107b1be7">
      <data key="d3">Every loan taken from the policy reduces the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, and those funds are subsequently directed into the Money Market sub-account as part of the policy’s internal fund management.</data>
      <data key="d4">causes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="d4b0a171-5ef3-4669-b3a4-2f4f8afafc3d">
      <data key="d3">A loan taken against the policy directly diminishes the death benefit by the same dollar amount borrowed. This reduction occurs independently of any proportional adjustment tied to withdrawal amounts.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="3ee62998-6a05-40f4-921c-b0c4be892b48">
      <data key="d3">The presence of a death benefit based on face amount plus accumulation means that reductions from loans directly affect the overall benefit, making the effect of loans particularly relevant under Option II.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="67add080-3372-4301-bd74-0e6a0c67fa5b">
      <data key="d3">Loans decrease the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, meaning they directly reduce the account value portion of the death benefit described in fact_sentence_1, thereby diminishing the overall payout.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces value</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.87</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="15d5a489-4261-4d61-bf32-69164a87bed6">
      <data key="d3">Policy loans reduce the death benefit dollar-for-dollar, meaning any reduction caused by borrowing diminishes the financial cushion needed to absorb the recurring 0.90% M&amp;E charge. This creates a feedback loop where higher borrowing accelerates erosion of the account value, making it harder to sustain sufficient reserves against the persistent cost of the M&amp;E charge.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6" target="2ac01dcd-f72c-441c-8596-afeaf23404ad">
      <data key="d3">Policy loans diminish the death benefit directly, making it harder for the remaining account value to sustain the daily M&amp;E charge without further premium contributions, potentially increasing strain on policy solvency.</data>
      <data key="d4">exacerbates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="75e7ac77-32c7-4164-a38e-032fec369745" target="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6">
      <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
      <data key="d4">reduced by loan</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="d9e45578-69a2-46fc-b85d-cfb150b9c7ea" target="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6">
      <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
      <data key="d4">reduced by loan</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="627eba8c-92a5-4b0c-ba53-08f6892c5122" target="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6">
      <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
      <data key="d4">directly causes</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="9eafaab8-8346-4d69-985f-8687f794e481" target="280ab5d3-6e4f-4198-81c8-a2ef27b919d6">
